     "text": "```python\ndef int_to_roman(num: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Convert an integer to a Roman numeral.\n\n    This function follows the latest coding standards and conventions established by LEAP, including\n    consistent naming, clear documentation, and adherence to PEP 8 guidelines. It ensures that\n    the code is readable, maintainable, and aligns with the team's coding practices.\n\n    Args:\n        num (int): The integer to be converted to a Roman numeral.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The Roman numeral representation of the input integer.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If the input is not a positive integer between 1 and 3999.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate input\n    if not isinstance(num, int) or num < 1 or num > 3999:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be an integer between 1 and 3999.\")\n\n    # Define the value-symbol pairs for Roman numerals\n    val_sym = [\n        (1000, 'M'),\n        (900, 'CM'),\n        (500, 'D'),\n        (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'),\n        (90, 'XC'),\n        (50, 'L'),\n        (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'),\n        (9, 'IX'),\n        (5, 'V'),\n        (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n\n    # Build the Roman numeral string\n    roman_num = ''\n    for value, symbol in val_sym:\n        while num >= value:\n            roman_num += symbol\n            num -= value\n\n    return roman_num\n```",
