     "text": "```python\ndef int_to_roman(num: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts an integer to a Roman numeral.\n    \n    The function uses a systematic approach to map integers to their corresponding Roman numeral representations.\n    It leverages a list of value-symbol pairs, iterating through them to construct the Roman numeral string.\n    The algorithm ensures that the correct symbols are appended in the right order, based on the value of the integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    val_sym = [\n        (1000, 'M'), (900, 'CM'), (500, 'D'), (400, 'CD'),\n        (100, 'C'), (90, 'XC'), (50, 'L'), (40, 'XL'),\n        (10, 'X'), (9, 'IX'), (5, 'V'), (4, 'IV'),\n        (1, 'I')\n    ]\n    \n    result = []\n    \n    for value, symbol in val_sym:\n        while num >= value:\n            result.append(symbol)\n            num -= value\n    \n    return ''.join(result)\n```",
